# Servora Web — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to .env.local for local overrides:
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Every value has a safe default (see src/lib/env.ts). Marketplace
# content (categories, providers, reviews) still runs entirely on local
# mock data (src/data/) — only authentication (src/lib/auth/) talks to a
# real backend, through the API Gateway below.
#
# Only NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed variables belong in this file. Anything
# without that prefix would be server-only and must never hold a secret
# that also needs client access — Next.js inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* values
# into the browser bundle at build time, so never put credentials, API
# keys, or tokens behind that prefix.

# Public site origin, used for absolute URLs in metadata (Open Graph,
# canonical links). Defaults to the production domain when unset.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://servora.hemandu.com

# The public Servora API Gateway origin. The browser only ever calls
# this — never servora-auth or any other downstream service directly.
# Defaults to the currently deployed gateway.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://servora-api-gateway.onrender.com

# Google OAuth 2.0 Client ID, used client-side to initialize Google
# Identity Services for "Continue with Google". This is the PUBLIC
# client ID — safe to ship to the browser. It must be the exact same
# Client ID servora-auth's GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID env var uses server-side to
# verify the token, or every Google sign-in will fail with
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_FAILED (wrong audience). Never put the Google client
# SECRET here or anywhere in this repo — that's servora-auth-only, and
# has no legitimate reason to exist in a frontend.
# Uncomment and set for Google auth to appear; the button hides itself
# when this is unset rather than initializing with a blank client ID.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
